    {
      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is an OIML Certificate from NMi (Netherlands):\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 5\n- Project number: 2496280\n- Page 1 of 11 (and pages 2, 3 shown)\n- Member State: The Netherlands\n- Scheme: A\n- Recommendation: R 49-1 (2013) \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water\"\n- Accuracy class: 1 and 2\n- Issuing authority: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- Date issued: 23 November 2021\n\nApplicant and Manufacturer: ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n\nCertified type: electromagnetic water meter\nTypes: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\n- X being 1 or 3 (standard or advanced)\n- Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\n- Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore)\n\nTest reports:\n- No. NMi-2254799-01 dated 14 August 2020, 235 pages\n- No. NMi-2254799-03 dated 30 September 2020, 44 pages\n- No. NMi-2254799-04 dated 24 December 2020, 180 pages\n- No. NMi-2564429-01 dated 02 August 2021, 26 pages\n- No. NMi-2496280-01 dated 26 August 2021, 29 pages\n- No. NMi-2496280-02 dated 23 November 2021, 25 pages\n\nDocumentation folder no. T11851-5 — this is documentation, could be test_reports with role documentation_file. The rule says test_reports role: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. The construction is recorded in Documentation folder no. T11851-5. I'll add it as a test_report entry with role documentation_file.\n\nCharacteristics Table 1 (general, type_level):\n- Measuring principle: Electromagnetic → measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- Accuracy class: 1 and 2 → accuracy_classes in recommendation; also could be a characteristic\n- Environmental class: O (installed outdoors) → environmental_class: \"O\"\n- Electromagnetic environment: E2 → electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- Mechanical class: M1 → mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- Temperature range ambient: -25 °C / +55 °C → {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n- Water temperature class: T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C) → water_temperature_class: \"T50\", maybe also range\n- Maximum admissible pressure (MAP): 1.6 MPa (16 bar) → maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- Orientation: All positions (Horizontal, vertical, or diagonal) → orientation\n- Flow profile sensitivity class: U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream), except for full bore sizes DN250 and larger: U3 and D0 → text value; this is config-dependent... Could be config_level with axis \"bore size\" but simpler keep as string at type level.\n- Reverse flow: sensor intended to measure reverse flow → reverse_flow: true or string\n- Pressure loss class: depends on size and bore — this is config_level. Reduced bore: DN40/DN50: Δp 40 (0.40 bar); larger than DN50: Δp 63 (0.63 bar). (Virtual) Full bore: DN40/DN50: Δp 10 (0.10 bar); DN65–DN200: Δp 16 (0.16 bar); DN250 and larger: Δp 10 (0.10 bar).\n- Power supply: AC mains: 85–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz; Renewable energy power (DC powered): 6–32 V; Replaceable Battery: 2x 3.6 V (operating range: 2.9-3.6 V)\n- Software identification: three versions with checksums\n- Checking facilities: Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\n\nRecommendation: R 49-1 (2013) → id \"R49\", edition... Hmm. R 49-1 (2013). The canonical id: \"R49\". Edition: 2013? The schema says edition (int). R49-1 2013 edition is 2013. I'll put edition: 2013. Amendment: null (not present). Scheme: A. Accuracy classes: [1, 2] — schema says accuracy_classes[] — as ints or strings? Probably [\"1\", \"2\"] or [1, 2]. I'll use [1, 2] or [\"1\",\"2\"]. Classes for water meters are 1 and 2. I'll use numbers... Hmm, accuracy classes are usually strings like \"1\", \"2\". I'll use strings to be safe? The R60 example in the system prompt doesn't show. I'll use [1, 2] as integers... Actually accuracy class values are commonly noted as strings. Let me use [\"1\", \"2\"]. Hmm. I'll go with [1, 2] numbers — no, strings safer for class labels. Let me decide: accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"].\n\nTable 2: General characteristics of the family of instruments — Reduced bore - AC mains powered.\n\nColumns: Meter size, Accuracy class, Q1 (min), Q2 (transitional), Q3 (permanent), Q4 (overload), Ratio Q3/Q1.\n\nThis is a matrix table with models being meter sizes × accuracy class. This is a model family.\n\nRows (converting decimal commas):\n- DN40, class 1: Q1=0.05, Q2=0.08, Q3=25, Q4=31.25, ratio 500\n- DN40, class 2: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.04, Q3=25, Q4=31.25, ratio 1000\n- DN50, class 1: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n- DN50, class 2: 0.04, 0.064, 40, 50, 1000\n- DN65, 1&2: 0.063, 0.1008, 63, 78.75, 1000\n- DN80, 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 100, 125, 1000\n- DN100, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN100, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN125, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN125, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN150, 1&2: 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000\n- DN200, 1&2: 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000\n- DN250, 1: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n- DN250, 2: 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000\n- DN300, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN300, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN350, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN350, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN400, 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN450, 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN500, 1: 8, 12.8, 4000, 5000, 500\n- DN500, 2: 4, 6.4, 4000, 5000, 1000\n- DN600, 1: 12.6, 20.16, 6300, 7875, 500\n- DN600, 2: 6.3, 10.08, 6300, 7875, 1000\n\nThis is a big matrix. The table has model axis = meter size (and accuracy class is a column attribute, not model). Note: the models should probably be meter sizes: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600.\n\nFor model_level characteristics, flow rates vary by size AND by accuracy class. So model_level would need model ids like \"DN40/class 1\". Hmm. The models are meter sizes, but flow rates differ per accuracy class within a size. So model_id could be \"DN40\" and then values conditioned on class... The schema: model_level entries have attribute, unit, values[]: {model, value}. I could create model ids like \"DN40 (class 1)\" and \"DN40 (class 2)\".\n\nAlternatively, keep the full matrix in matrix_tables and define model_level with model ids \"DN40-class1\" etc. Since sizes DN65, DN80, DN150, DN200, DN400, DN450 have same values for both classes, and others differ.\n\nI think the cleanest: model_family.models = meter sizes (15 entries), matrix_tables preserves the full table (24 rows with accuracy class column), and model_level entries use model ids combining size and class where needed... That would be inconsistent.\n\nGiven the complexity, maybe define models as size+class combos only where flow rates differ? Hmm.\n\nActually, the fundamental model axis is the meter size; accuracy class is an attribute. For attributes like q1 that vary by both, I could use model_id \"DN40\" and value as... no, can't have two values per model.\n\nOption: use config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\" for q1, q2 (the class-dependent ones), and model_level for q3, q4, ratio? But ratio also varies by class for some sizes (DN40: 500 vs 1000; DN100: 500 vs 1000; DN500: 500 vs 1000; DN600: 500 vs 1000).\n\nLet me think about what's most faithful: matrix_tables with all 24 rows, and model_level where the model axis is size+class. Actually, since Table 2 heading says \"Reduced bore - AC mains powered\", there are probably more tables on later pages (full bore, DC powered, battery) — so the axes are: bore type × power supply × size × class. The models in later parts probably defined by these tables. For this fragment, I'll define models by size, and handle class-dependence via config_level axis \"accuracy_class\".\n\nPlan for model_level:\n- q1: config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\"? Or model_level with model \"DN40 (class 1)\". Hmm.\n\nThe schema says model_level[]: attribute, unit, values[]: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}. Config_level[]: attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}.\n\nBest approach:\n- model_level for attributes that depend only on size: q3 (permanent flow rate), q4 (overload flow rate). q3: DN40: 25, DN50: 40, DN65: 63, DN80: 100, DN100: 160, DN125: 160, DN150: 400, DN200: 630, DN250: 1000, DN300: 1600, DN350: 1600, DN400: 2500, DN450: 2500, DN500: 4000, DN600: 6300. q4 similarly.\n- config_level for attributes that depend on accuracy class (q1, q2, q3/q1 ratio): axis \"accuracy_class\", conditions \"class 1\" and \"class 2\" per size... but config_level values are {condition, value} where condition describes the config. For q1, the value depends on BOTH size and class. That's a 2-D thing that doesn't fit model_level (1 model axis) nor config_level (1 config axis) cleanly.\n\nGiven the messiness, the matrix_tables entry preserves everything — that's the primary mechanism for multi-dim tables (\"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\").\n\nFor model_level, I'll use model ids that combine size and class: e.g., \"DN40 class 1\", \"DN40 class 2\", etc. That gives 24 model variants for this table. But model_family.models should then list... hmm, that's a lot. But it's the most faithful.\n\nAlternatively, simpler: model_family.models = sizes only; matrix_tables preserves the table; model_level includes q3, q4 per size; and q1, q2, ratio as config_level with axis \"size × accuracy class\"? That's weird too.\n\nActually the R137 example: sizes G1.6, G2.5 as models, with qmax, qt, qmin per model. Here the analog: size is the model, and accuracy class is an extra dimension. For attributes varying by class, I could use config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\" and conditions like \"DN40, class 1\" → value 0.05. Actually config_level condition is free text: {condition, value}. So:\n\nconfig_level:\n- attribute \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\", axis \"meter size and accuracy class\", values: [{condition: \"DN40, class 1\", value: {…0.05 m³/h}}, {condition: \"DN40, class 2\", value: 0.025}, ...]\n\nThat's 24 entries per attribute — verbose but faithful. Hmm, but this replicates the matrix table entirely. Maybe better to just keep matrix_tables and populate model_level for the size-only-dependent attributes (q3, q4), and leave q1/q2/ratio in the matrix table plus maybe config_level entries.\n\nI think a pragmatic approach: since the fragment explicitly is \"Table 2 General characteristics of the family of instruments\", emit matrix_tables with the full table, model_family with models = sizes (DN40...DN600, 15 sizes), model_level for q3 and q4 (depend only on size), and config_level for q1, q2, q3_q1_ratio with axis \"accuracy_class\" and conditions combining size+class, e.g. condition \"DN40, class 1\". Hmm, but then value would be a number with unit m³/h.\n\nActually wait — for class-dependent attributes, conditions like \"DN40 class 1\" encode size, which duplicates the model axis. Alternatively treat model axis as size and note per-model values that are objects {class1: ..., class2: ...}? The schema doesn't support that.\n\nLet me just go with model ids = \"DN40 class 1\" style for the class-dependent attributes in config_level... no.\n\nDecision: I'll do this:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"AquaMaster4\" (the type family name — actually type is \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4\")\n- models: 15 entries with model_id = \"DN40\" ... \"DN600\" and attributes? The schema allows attributes{} per model. I could put flow rates in model attributes but class-dependence again. I'll keep attributes minimal: none, or maybe note.\n\nmatrix_tables: one table \"General characteristics of the family of instruments — Reduced bore, AC mains powered\" with columns [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1_minimum\", \"q2_transitional\", \"q3_permanent\", \"q4_overload\", \"q3_q1_ratio\"], column_units [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null], rows = 24 rows as objects.\n\ncharacteristics.model_level:\n- q3 permanent flow rate: 15 models\n- q4 overload flow rate: 15 models\n\ncharacteristics.config_level:\n- minimum flow rate q1: axis \"accuracy class\", values per size+class? 24 entries. Hmm heavy. Alternatively axis \"meter size and accuracy class\".\n\nActually, you know, maybe cleaner: config_level entries with axis \"accuracy_class\" where condition = \"DN40, class 1\" etc. It's verbose but correct. Or I could just include the matrix table and skip config_level for these, since the matrix table preserves it. The rules say matrix_tables is for \"multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". q1/q2/ratio don't fit cleanly elsewhere, so leaving them in the matrix table is acceptable. But q3/q4 do fit model_level.\n\nI'll include config_level for q1, q2, and ratio to be thorough. Hmm, 24 conditions × 3 attributes = 72 entries. That's a lot of JSON but it's faithful. Alternatively, note that for several sizes (DN65, DN80, DN150, DN200, DN400, DN450) class 1 & 2 share the same row (labeled \"1 & 2\"). For those, one condition \"class 1 & 2\" applies.\n\nLet me reconsider: q1 values by (size, class):\n- DN40: c1 0.05, c2 0.025\n- DN50: c1 0.08, c2 0.04\n- DN65: both 0.063\n- DN80: both 0.1\n- DN100: c1 0.32, c2 0.16\n- DN125: c1 0.32, c2 0.16\n- DN150: both 0.4\n- DN200: both 0.63\n- DN250: c1 2, c2 1\n- DN300: c1 3.2, c2 1.6\n- DN350: c1 3.2, c2 1.6\n- DN400: both 5\n- DN450: both 5\n- DN500: c1 8, c2 4\n- DN600: c1 12.6, c2 6.3\n\nInteresting: for the \"1 & 2\" rows, the ratio is 1000; for split rows, class 1 ratio 500, class 2 ratio 1000. Q1 = Q3/ratio. So q1 could be expressed as formula Q3/(Q3/Q1). Not needed.\n\nI'll emit config_level with axis \"accuracy class (per meter size)\" and 24 conditions for q1... Actually let me count entries: q1: 24, q2: 24, ratio: 24. 72 config entries. JSON will be long but fine.\n\nHmm, alternatively, I can reduce by using config_level per attribute with axis \"meter size × accuracy class\". I'll do it.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the model definition. Maybe models should be size+class combos? In R137 example, models were sizes because the whole row differed by size. Here each row is size+class. The meter \"model\" in the real certificate is like \"AquaMaster4 FEW4...\" with DN size being a variant parameter. Accuracy class is a property of the meter variant too.\n\nI think models = sizes is the most natural reading of \"family of instruments\" by meter size. And class-dependence handled via config_level. Good.\n\nNow Table 1 type-level characteristics:\n\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\" (string value)\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" — already in recommendation.accuracy_classes; skip as characteristic? Could include as type_level: accuracy_class value \"1 and 2\". I'll include it maybe as it's in Table 1. Hmm, schema has accuracy_classes under recommendation. I'll put it there and also could skip in characteristics. I'll include in characteristics too? To avoid redundancy, put in recommendation only. Actually the instruction: don't fabricate, but redundancy isn't fabrication. I'll keep it just in recommendation to keep clean. Hmm, but Table 1 lists it as a characteristic. I'll include accuracy_classes in recommendation and not in characteristics.\n\n- environmental_class: value \"O (installed outdoors)\" → value: \"O\", plus note? I'll do value \"O\" with maybe an attribute note... Keep value: \"O\" and a separate note not possible. I'll use value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"? The structured value is {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. value can be string. I'll put \"O\". Hmm, losing \"installed outdoors\" info. Maybe environmental_class: {value: \"O\"} and add type_level \"environment_description\"? Simpler: value \"O (installed outdoors)\". I'll go with \"O (installed outdoors)\" as string — wait, canonical labels rule says no trailing punctuation but content strings are fine. I'll use value: \"O (installed outdoors)\".\n\nActually cleaner: environmental_class: \"O\", environmental_class_note: \"installed outdoors\"? That's fabricating a label. I'll just keep the full string.\n\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C. Label: temperature_range_ambient → canonical \"ambient_temperature_range\". The label in doc: \"Temperature range ambient\". Canonical: ambient_temperature_range. Add note \"(for both flow sensor and flow transmitter)\" — could be a footnote-ish; I'll include as separate characteristic? Or keep as part of... The structured value only has value/unit/markers. I'll add a type_level characteristic \"temperature_range_scope\"? That's fabricating. I could include it in the value as string? No — value must be numeric range. I'll drop the parenthetical or put it in matrix description... I'll just omit it; it's fine. Actually I could add it as description somewhere. Not critical.\n\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\" and range +0.1 to +50 °C. I'll add water_temperature_class: \"T50\" and water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit °C. The doc: \"T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\". Adding a derived characteristic is somewhat fabrication but reasonable. I'll include both: water_temperature_class value \"T50\"; and maybe skip range to avoid fabrication? The range is explicitly in the doc. I'll include water_temperature_range {min 0.1, max 50} °C.\n\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6, unit \"MPa\". Also \"(16 bar)\" — secondary. I'll include maximum_admissible_pressure {1.6 MPa}. Could add note. Maybe add \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": 16? That's derived but present. Hmm. I'll include just MPa one, and maybe a second with bar. I'll include both? The label \"(MAP)\". Canonical label: maximum_admissible_pressure. I'll do value 1.6 unit MPa. I'll skip the 16 bar (redundant).\n\nActually, to preserve info, I could put value: 1.6, unit \"MPa\" and that's it.\n\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\" → value string \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\". Keep.\n\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: string \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream), except full bore sizes DN250 and larger: U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\". This is config-dependent (full bore DN250+). Could be config_level with axis \"bore type and size\". I could put type_level value as the general \"U0 and D0\" and config_level for DN250+ full bore. Let me do:\n  - type_level flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"\n  - config_level: attribute flow_profile_sensitivity_class, axis \"bore type and size\", condition \"full bore, DN250 and larger\", value \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\".\n  \n  That's a good use of config_level.\n\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → value true? I'll set value: true. Or string. I'll use true with label reverse_flow_capability. Keep label reverse_flow, value true. Hmm \"true\" as boolean — value can be number|string|range. Boolean not listed. I'll use string \"yes\"? Better: value: \"intended to measure reverse flow\"? I'll use value: true — schema says <number | {min,max} | string>. Use \"true\" string? I'll just put value: \"sensor intended to measure reverse flow\". Safe.\n\n- pressure_loss_class: config-dependent (bore type × size). Use config_level with axis \"bore type and size\":\n  - condition \"reduced bore, DN40–DN50\": \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\" — value as class \"Δp 40\", with pressure 0.40 bar. The structured value: I could give value \"Δp 40\" string and separate pressure characteristic. Hmm. Pressure loss class is a class designation (Δp 40). I'll record config_level attribute pressure_loss_class with value \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"? Or record two attributes: pressure_loss_class (string) and maximum_pressure_loss (numeric bar).\n  \n  Let me do config_level entries:\n  - attribute pressure_loss_class, axis \"bore type and meter size\":\n    - condition \"reduced bore, DN40–DN50\": \"Δp 40\" / 0.40 bar\n    - condition \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\": \"Δp 63\" / 0.63 bar\n    - condition \"(virtual) full bore, DN40–DN50\": \"Δp 10\" / 0.10 bar\n    - condition \"(virtual) full bore, DN65–DN200\": \"Δp 16\" / 0.16 bar\n    - condition \"(virtual) full bore, DN250 and larger\": \"Δp 10\" / 0.10 bar\n  \n  The value objects: {condition, value, unit_symbol}? Schema for config_level values: {condition, value} — rule 5 says values follow same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. So I can include unit_symbol. For pressure_loss_class, value \"Δp 40\" no unit; and maybe also attribute maximum_pressure_loss with numeric values 0.40 bar etc.\n\n  I'll do both attributes: pressure_loss_class (string values) and maximum_pressure_loss (numeric, unit bar). Wait \"Δp 40\" means Δp = 0.40 bar? Δp 40 → 0.40 bar; Δp 63 → 0.63 bar; Δp 10 → 0.10 bar; Δp 16 → 0.16 bar. Yes, class number /100 = bar.\n\n- power supply: multiple characteristics:\n  - power_supply_type: \"AC mains\" → but there are three power supply options. This is config-dependent (power supply variant). Config_level axis \"power supply\":\n    - condition \"AC mains\": voltage 85–240 V AC, frequency 50/60 Hz\n    - condition \"renewable energy power (DC powered)\": 6–32 V DC\n    - condition \"replaceable battery\": 2x 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V)\n  \n  I'll do config_level:\n  - attribute power_supply_voltage, axis \"power supply option\", conditions:\n    - \"AC mains\": {min:85, max:240} V\n    - \"DC powered (renewable energy)\": {min:6, max:32} V\n    - \"replaceable battery\": 3.6 V (2 cells) — battery: \"2x 3.6 V\" per cell voltage. value 3.6 V, condition \"replaceable battery (2 cells)\". And battery operating range 2.9–3.6 V as separate attribute battery_operating_voltage_range.\n  - attribute power_supply_frequency, condition \"AC mains\": {min:50,max:60} Hz.\n  - attribute battery_cell_count? condition... maybe fold into condition string.\n\n  Alternatively type_level with combined strings — no, rules prefer splitting. Config_level is right.\n\n- software_identification: three versions with checksums. Type_level? It's a list. Could be config_level axis \"software version\": conditions \"03.00.03\" → checksum B26AFE6B, etc. I'll do:\n  - type_level? software_versions as list of strings? Better config_level:\n    attribute software_checksum, axis \"software version\", values: [{condition: \"03.00.03\", value: \"B26AFE6B\"}, {condition: \"03.00.04\", value: \"6E98963A\"}, {condition: \"03.02.00\", value: \"E12E5C04\"}]\n\n- checking_facilities (Table on page 3): \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\" → type_level checking_facility: \"Type P (permanent) automatic\". Label checking_facilities → checking_facility_type. Value string.\n\nNow the type designations: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\" and \"FET4 *\". With wildcard explanation: X ∈ {1,3}, Y ∈ {1,2,8,9}, Z ∈ {R,V,F}. So type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4\"]. The asterisk note: \"* With X being 1 or 3...\" — this is a wildcard definition, could go in description or footnotes. It's not a footnote per se (no numbered marker), but it explains the \"*\". I'll include in certified_type.description and maybe footnotes with marker \"*\"? Footnotes schema: marker, text. The \"*\" is a marker of sorts. I'll add footnote: marker \"*\", text \"With X being 1 or 3 (standard or advanced), Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only) and Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\" That works nicely.\n\nCategory: \"Water meter\" (per schema example list: \"Water meter\" not listed but R49 is water meters; category examples include \"Measuring system\", \"Gas meter\"... I'll use \"Water meter\").\n\nComponents: flow sensor and flow transmitter are mentioned (temperature range \"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\"). Type FEW = flow sensor? FET = ? Actually AquaMaster4: FEW4 is the flow sensor... wait. Hmm: FEW4XY.Z — FEW could be \"flow electronik water\"? FET4 = flow ... transmitter? The certificate says types \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". It mentions \"flow sensor and flow transmitter\" in temperature range. Possibly FEW4 = sensor+transmitter integral, FET4 = remote transmitter? Not clearly defined. I should be careful not to fabricate. The markdown doesn't explicitly assign FEW4=sensor. I'll skip components[] since roles aren't explicitly defined. Actually \"integral/remote version\" for Y. I'll not fabricate component roles. Skip components.\n\nCertificate object:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"2496280\"\n- page_total: 11\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2021-11-23\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n- revision: \"5\"? Schema doesn't have revision at certificate level but revision_history is for cert revisions. The number includes \"revision 5\". Hmm, revision_history[]: {revision: \"5\", date: \"2021-11-23\", changes: null}? We don't know change details. Could include revision_history entry: revision \"5\", date 23 November 2021 (issue date). Changes unknown — omit changes. I'll include it since revision is stated. Actually the \"revision 5\" refers to certificate revision. I'll add revision_history: [{revision: \"5\", date: \"2021-11-23\"}]. Hmm, date might be original issue date of revision 5 = 23 Nov 2021 (the date signed). Reasonable. Or omit. I'll include with date.\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"\n- person_title: null (not given; \"Certification Board\" is a section header, not title). Omit.\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: